  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Research Innovation in Estimating the Prevalence of Human Trafficking in the Costa Rican Fishing Sector</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The New York University and John Jay College of Criminal Justice in collaboration with IOM as an implementer partner will conduct a prevalence study in the fishing industry of Costa Rica, funded by the U.S. Department of State Office to Monitor and Combat Trafficking in Persons (J/TiP). 
 
This joint study will investigate prevalence techniques for cases of human trafficking by comparing the robustness, cost and energy efficiency of two different data collection methods. Current methods for human trafficking prevalence research are often inadequate to understand this crime that frequently affects irregular migrants, a difficult to reach population. Determining reliable and efficient data collection strategies is critical to future investigations which will in turn support human trafficking prevention measures, victim protection and criminal prosecutions. 
 
As suggested by the J/TIP call for proposals, this study will focus on Costa Rica&#x2019;s fishing sector. Fishing in Costa Rica is characterized by decentralized artisanal fisheries that are not consistently registered with the government. Given these logistical challenges to data collection, fishing communities would be a highly informative population for comparing the robustness of different methodologies; any methodology identified as cost-effective and comprehensive in this challenging population will be useful across a range of sectors.</narrative>
  </description>
